<h2>From missing-person report to conviction</h2>
<p>The case began in March 2021, when a mother reported that her 13-year-old daughter had gone missing from her home in Delhi. The child was recovered on 27 March 2021 from premises in JJ Colony, Wazirpur. The prosecution&rsquo;s case was that the appellant — a man the family trusted, described in the evidence as the victim&rsquo;s <em>dharam bhai</em> — had taken her away and sexually assaulted her over a period of time. The trial court convicted him on 1 April 2025 and, on 15 April 2025, sentenced him to twenty years&rsquo; rigorous imprisonment under Section 6 POCSO, with concurrent terms under Sections 363 and 506 IPC, fines, and victim compensation of Rs 10,50,000 under Section 33(8) POCSO read with Rule 9 of the POCSO Rules, 2020.</p>

<h2>The three questions on appeal</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><h3>1. Could the sole testimony sustain conviction?</h3><p>The defence attacked the victim&rsquo;s uncorroborated account and pleaded false implication arising from a monetary dispute.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>2. Was minority proved?</h3><p>The age finding rested on the school record showing a date of birth of 26 May 2008 — about 12 years 9 months at the relevant time.</p></div>
  <div class="fstep"><h3>3. Did investigative lapses vitiate the case?</h3><p>Date discrepancies around the missing complaint and recovery, and other lapses, were pressed as fatal defects.</p></div>
</div>

<h2>The Court\'s answers</h2>
<p>All three questions were answered against the appellant. On sole testimony, the Court held that &ldquo;the testimony of a prosecutrix, if trustworthy and inspiring confidence, does not require corroboration as a matter of law,&rdquo; and found that &ldquo;no material contradiction or infirmity has emerged in her testimony so as to discredit her version.&rdquo; On age, the school record stood proved and unimpeached. On the investigation, the Court reiterated the settled position that defective investigation is not by itself a ground to discard otherwise reliable evidence — a principle that prevents the prosecution&rsquo;s procedural failings from automatically becoming the accused&rsquo;s windfall where the substantive evidence holds. The false-implication plea, said to arise from a monetary dispute, &ldquo;remains unsupported by any material on record.&rdquo;</p>

<h2>Why the offence was aggravated</h2>
<p>The Court held that the proved conduct fell within Sections 5(l) and 5(n) of the POCSO Act — repeated assault, and assault by a person in a position of trust — attracting the aggravated offence under Section 6. The relationship of trust that gave the appellant access to the child was thus not merely background: it was an element that elevated the offence and fixed the sentencing floor. Since the twenty-year term was the statutory minimum for the aggravated offence, the Court found &ldquo;no ground to interfere with the sentence imposed.&rdquo; The convictions, concurrent sentences, fines and the compensation direction were all upheld, with set-off under Section 428 CrPC for the period already served.</p>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice perspective:</strong> POCSO appeals increasingly turn on two fixed points — the statutory proof of age and the quality of the child&rsquo;s testimony. Where the school record survives scrutiny and the child&rsquo;s account is consistent on the core allegation, arguments built on peripheral discrepancies and investigative lapses rarely succeed. Defence strategy has to engage the core evidence at trial, through focused cross-examination conducted within the limits the law sets for child witnesses; an appellate court will not reweigh a credible child witness merely because the investigation could have been better. Equally, the judgment is a reminder that compensation under Section 33(8) and Rule 9 is now a standard component of POCSO sentencing in Delhi, and appellate affirmation includes it.</p>
</div>
